WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.570
My robot was meant to stop at the door.

2
00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:05.460
Instead it walked straight into

3
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.500
the wall, every single time.

4
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.000
My pupils groaned, then one of them

5
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.460
shouted: it’s a bug, sir, let’s find it!

6
00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:16.770
Today: decisions, and how

7
00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.110
to find and fix bugs.

8
00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:20.970
Programs make decisions.

9
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.180
If it is raining, then take an

10
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.460
umbrella, else take a hat.

11
00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.810
The if part is a condition: a

12
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.130
question whose answer is yes or no.

13
00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.210
The answer decides which steps run.

14
00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.590
Some decisions need two conditions.

15
00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.360
The classroom fan goes on if it is

16
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.080
hot AND someone is in the room.

17
00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.710
The alarm rings if the door

18
00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:49.430
OR the window opens.

19
00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.160
AND needs both; OR needs at least one.

20
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.560
You can teach decisions

21
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.040
with no device at all.

22
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.640
Draw a flowchart on the board: boxes for

23
00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.980
actions, a diamond for each question.

24
00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:06.350
Pupils follow it with their fingers.

25
00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:10.770
Then play Simon says, with ifs: if

26
00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.610
you are wearing blue, stand up.

27
00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:14.550
Now, bugs.

28
00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.970
A bug is a mistake in a program that

29
00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.350
makes it do the wrong thing.

30
00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.580
Debugging is finding and fixing it.

31
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.040
Every programmer writes bugs, every day.

32
00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.470
The skill isn’t avoiding them;

33
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.370
it’s finding them calmly.

34
00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:32.250
Quick check!

35
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.470
My robot should go forward two,

36
00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.090
turn right, forward one.

37
00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.420
My program says forward two,

38
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.080
turn left, forward one.

39
00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:45.870
Which card is the bug?

40
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.920
The second card: it turns

41
00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.320
left instead of right.

42
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.360
Notice how we found it.

43
00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.610
We compared what we wanted with what

44
00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.530
happened, card by card, and stopped

45
00:01:58.530 --> 00:01:59.730
at the first difference.

46
00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:02.830
Give pupils a routine they

47
00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.350
can follow every time.

48
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.490
Test the program and watch what happens.

49
00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.720
Find the first step that goes wrong.

50
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.300
Change one thing only.

51
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.520
Then test again, and

52
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:16.880
repeat until it works.

53
00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.310
Make bugs normal in your classroom.

54
00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.700
Celebrate a bug found, not

55
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.120
just a program that works.

56
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.660
Say I made a bug, out loud,

57
00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:29.780
when you make one.

58
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.780
Pupils who aren’t afraid

59
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.360
of bugs keep trying.

60
00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.970
In the app, open Logic gates.

61
00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:39.990
Flip switches to learn AND and

62
00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.150
OR, then solve the Fan.

63
00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:44.990
Each cross in the table is a case

64
00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:47.730
your gates get wrong: change one

65
00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.610
gate at a time, and test again.

66
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:51.550
Your turn.

67
00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.090
A pupil’s robot crashes, and she wants

68
00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:56.650
to rewrite the whole program.

69
00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:58.460
What should she do first?

70
00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.810
Find the first wrong step.

71
00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.780
Remember: a decision runs steps

72
00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.860
only if a condition is true.

73
00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:11.070
AND needs both conditions; OR needs one.

74
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.720
Debugging is a routine: test, find,

75
00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.120
change one thing, test again.

76
00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.730
Try it in class tomorrow: bug swap.

77
00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.430
Pairs write a robot program on

78
00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.670
paper with one bug hidden in it.

79
00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:28.650
They swap, find the other pair’s bug,

80
00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.730
and explain how they found it.
